{
  "requirement": "`habeas::validate_repository(root)` MUST return a report with the number of discovered requirements, the number of evidence files, and all validation errors for the supplied project. Ordinary invalid evidence MUST be reported in that report; an inaccessible project root MUST return an I/O error.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met. The implementation is complete and the tests cover it. One small gap is the extra `examples` field, which I judged benign.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `habeas::validate_repository` is re-exported from `src/lib.rs`. Its definition (`src/evidence.rs:119`) returns `io::Result<EvidenceReport>`. It delegates to `inspect_repository(root)?.report`, so I/O failures propagate as errors and the report is returned otherwise.\n- `EvidenceReport` (`src/evidence.rs:16`) has `requirements`, `evidence_files` and `errors`. `inspect_repository_with_progress` (`src/evidence.rs:172`) fills them in:\n  - `requirements` counts definitions of kind `Requirement`.\n  - `evidence_files` is the number of evidence JSON files found.\n  - `errors` collects every validation error: configuration, definitions, evidence files, orphan and missing evidence, references, and Rust item coverage.\n- Ordinary invalid evidence does not abort inspection. Each problem is pushed into `errors` and processing continues. This covers invalid JSON, orphans, schema errors, stale text and hashes, and false determinations.\n- An inaccessible root fails at `fs::canonicalize(root)?`, which is the only early-return I/O failure. It surfaces as an `io::Error`.\n- All the code involved carries `{impl}` markers: the struct, `validate_repository`, `inspect_repository` and `inspect_repository_with_progress`.\n- The report also has an `examples` count, which this requirement does not mention. It is an additive field. It is used by `habeas::validate`'s failure message, and the named-examples feature (`requirements.examples.evidence`) is the other requirement this code implements. It does not contradict anything in the text, so I did not treat it as a gap.\n\n**Tests**\n- `inaccessible_project_root_is_an_io_error_not_a_report` (`tests/evidence_boundaries.rs:557`) checks that a missing root returns `Err` with kind `NotFound`. It also checks that invalid evidence in an accessible root is reported in `errors` rather than failing.\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` asserts `requirements == 1`, `evidence_files == 1` and no errors.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` checks that many different invalid-evidence kinds are all collected in one report.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` checks that the counts and errors are the same with and without progress reporting, in both valid and invalid repositories.\n- `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs:495` and the `tests/commands.rs` fixture exercise the public API on real project layouts, including external build outputs.\n- `cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates` and the span/fence tests also assert the `requirements`, `examples` and `evidence_files` counts.\n- Only the not-found case is tested for the I/O error. Other I/O failures, such as permission denied, all go through the same `?` on canonicalize. That is an acceptable gap.",
  "references": "b29e530bf260b1400a1b11fe3542a589c48af5d5952ad5be6bfe6199326d9394",
